py_test(name = "weld_reverted_content_staleness_test", srcs = ["weld_reverted_content_staleness_test.py", "_dirty_tree_lib.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld/strategies", "//weld/strategies:helpers"])  # bd 5038-lhye: ADR 0017's fourth amendment -- an edit that is discovered, then reverted to its committed content, clears every existing signal at once (git status clean, empty commit range, file already in the inventory), so the graph answers from content on no commit and in no working tree. reverted_content_stale reads the inventory directly, bounded by a stat precheck against discovery-state.json's own mtime rather than a new per-writer stamp, which is what answers bd 0jay's rejected-design objection: no writer other than discovery can move that reference point, so none can launder a divergence -- pinned here by a regression proving `wd add-node --merge`'s underlying call leaves discovery-state.json byte-identical
py_test(name = "weld_bzl_input_staleness_test", srcs = ["weld_bzl_input_staleness_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld/strategies", "//weld/strategies:helpers"])  # bd a4q8: the two suites above compare a dirty path against the ADR 0008 inventory, and the inventory was built from the glob-resolved source set alone -- so a file discovery genuinely reads but no glob resolves (a `.bzl` a BUILD file loads, which ADR 0109 evaluates into a target's real srcs) was recorded nowhere. Measured: editing one produced NO staleness signal at all, while discover.yaml's own bazel-entry comment still claimed it did, and an incremental run over the same edit answered "no files changed" and left the contains edge on the previous srcs. Real discovery over a BUILD + .bzl fixture, asserting the .bzl is in discovered_from first so the module cannot pass by describing nothing. The load-bearing assertion is that the settling discover RE-EXTRACTS the target: re-stamping the hash alone satisfies every freshness check here and still serves a graph built from content that is gone
py_test(name = "auto_refresh_settles_once_test", srcs = ["auto_refresh_settles_once_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime", "//weld/strategies", "//weld/strategies:helpers"])  # bd hmaz: the outcome bd o18k's process-local refresh cache used to deliver, restated over auto_refresh_if_stale after that cache was removed. o18k's premise was that the ADR 0017 working-tree dimension latched source_stale on any held edit, so auto-refresh re-ran discovery on every following read; bd 0jay moved the settle into the freshness signal, so the read path returns at its own staleness guard instead. Measured 0 cache hits in 60 reads (11 reaching the cache) before removal. Counts REAL discovery runs through the read path -- weld_dirty_worktree_settles_test pins the signal settling, this pins the work not repeating. Three assertions so it cannot pass for the wrong reason: exactly one run across repeated reads of an unchanging dirty tree, the graph reading fresh between them (else a swallowed refresh failure would satisfy the count), and a further edit re-running discovery (else an over-eager never-refresh would)
py_test(name = "discover_inputs_containment_test", srcs = ["discover_inputs_containment_test.py"], deps = ["//weld:runtime"])  # bd 566g: direct unit pin for weld._discover_inputs's untrusted-discovered_from containment (none existed -- _within_root and stale_directory_marker were previously covered only behaviorally through _discover_single_repo fixtures, bd 0t5p / bd a4q8 above). Both functions must refuse a '..' lexical escape and a symlink resolving outside root rather than stat the unresolved path, and both must still accept a same-tree symlink and an ordinary contained entry -- the read-side half of the write-side invariant weld_bazel_loads_containment_test guards. Mutation-verified during review: regressing either function to stat (root / entry) directly, without the resolve()+relative_to() guard, flips exactly the four escape-case assertions to failing while the two non-escape cases per function stay green

